.TH "GIT\-EXCLUDE" "1" "September 25, 2026" "git\-exclude" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git\-exclude \- manage .git/info/exclude from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]git exclude\f[R] [\-\-] <path>...
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]git exclude\f[R] (\f[B]remove\f[R]|\f[B]rm\f[R]) [\-\-] <path>...
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]git exclude\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]git exclude\f[R] [\f[B]\-h\f[R] | \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Keeps your own notes and working files in a repository without
committing them, by writing patterns to \f[I].git/info/exclude\f[R].
Think of them as notes scribbled in the margins of your own copy of a
book.
Adding them to \f[I].gitignore\f[R] would turn your clutter into noise
for everyone else, whereas every clone has its own local excludes file,
which lives with the repository and is never committed or shared.
.PP
With no arguments, opens the exclude file in the editor git would use
for a commit message, honouring \f[B]GIT_EDITOR\f[R],
\f[B]core.editor\f[R], \f[B]VISUAL\f[R] and \f[B]EDITOR\f[R] in that
order.
.PP
Given paths, appends one pattern per path.
Every pattern is anchored to the repository root, so running \f[B]git
exclude build\f[R] inside \f[I]src/\f[R] writes \f[I]/src/build\f[R] and
never matches a \f[I]build\f[R] elsewhere in the tree.
A leading \f[I]./\f[R] is dropped.
Absolute paths and paths containing \f[I]..\f[R] are refused, since the
pattern is written relative to the repository root and the path need not
exist.
Trailing slashes are stripped; git treats the anchored pattern as
matching either a file or a directory of that name.
The path does not have to exist yet.
A path that starts with \f[B]\-\f[R], or is named \f[B]remove\f[R] or
\f[B]rm\f[R], goes after \f[B]\-\-\f[R], as with git\(aqs own commands.
An empty path, a path containing a newline, an absolute path and a path
containing \f[B]..\f[R] are each refused before anything is written, as
is any use inside a bare repository, where an excludes file has no
meaning.
Each path is reported as \f[B]Excluded\f[R] or \f[B]Already
excluded\f[R], and is never written twice.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]remove\f[R] <path>..., \f[B]rm\f[R] <path>...
Removes the pattern that \f[B]git exclude\f[R] <path> would have
written, by exact line match, and reports \f[B]Removed\f[R].
Characters such as \f[B]*\f[R] and \f[B][\f[R] in a path are matched
literally.
If the path is hidden by some broader pattern that was not written for
it, nothing is removed and the command reports \f[B]Not excluded\f[R],
rather than un\-excluding everything else that pattern covers.
.PP
Lines are compared byte for byte, so a pattern an editor wrote with a
carriage return is not recognised as the same pattern and is neither
deduplicated nor removed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Prints a short usage summary.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Prints the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]$GIT_DIR/info/exclude\f[R]
In a linked worktree this resolves to the common git directory, which is
where git reads the file from.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]gitignore\f[R](5), \f[B]git\-var\f[R](1)
